Play speech responses that arrive as raw PCM

`/v1/audio/speech` does not oblige a provider to return a container, and several
OpenAI-compatible servers answer `audio/L16` — bare PCM16 samples, no header.
`getAudioResponseFormat` does not recognise those media types and falls through
to its `mp3` default, so the bytes reach the browser labelled as MP3. Every
consumer then fails identically, because `new Audio(URL.createObjectURL(blob))`
cannot decode them:

    TTS test failed: Failed to load because no supported source was found.

which is the browser's own message and says nothing about the cause. Measured
against such a server: `content-type: audio/L16;rate=24000;channels=1`, and the
first bytes carry neither a RIFF nor an ID3 magic.

This adds the missing step. `decodeAudioResponse` parses the media type for
`audio/l16`, `audio/pcm` and `audio/x-pcm` — taking `rate` and `channels` from
its parameters, defaulting to 24 kHz mono when they are absent — and prepends
the 44-byte RIFF/WAVE header that makes the same samples playable. Anything
already in a container is returned untouched.

Done here rather than at each player so that preview, regeneration and classroom
playback are fixed together, and so the rule stays in one place: everything past
this function is in a container. The three provider paths that read a response
body (OpenAI, Lemonade, VoxCPM) had identical five-line endings and now share it.

Verified two ways: five unit tests over the header fields, the media-type
parameters and the pass-through cases; and a real response from such a server
(16,384 bytes of L16) converted and then opened by an independent decoder —
Python's `wave` module reports 1 channel, 16-bit, 24 kHz, 8,192 frames.

